# Firefox add-ons (webextensions/README.md): Firefox's WebExtensions APIs that
# Chromium lacks. The functions are in dubna's src/extensions/; this registers them
# in their namespaces' schemas, builds them and registers their prefs. Firefox's
# names for Chromium's APIs are aliases: browser.menus and the "menus" permission
# are contextMenus. Firefox's Manifest V3 background pages (background.scripts or
# .page) run as Manifest V2's do, unless the manifest also has a service worker;
# runtime.getContexts() lists such a page as the background.
# A Firefox version with a suffix ("3.4resigned1") loads as its leading numbers,
# with the full string as the version name (firefox_version.cc).
# A background with "type": "module" loads its scripts as modules, as Firefox does.
# Locale files load as Firefox reads them: a message of any name ("first-install-body"),
# a placeholder the message doesn't define becomes empty, and homepage_url
# is localized too.
# declarative_net_request without its permission loads without its rulesets,
# as in Firefox.
# A tab that hasn't committed a navigation has the URL about:blank for a Firefox
# add-on (firefox_add_on.h), as in Firefox.
# Shortcuts load as Firefox reads them: Ctrl+Alt, F1 to F12 alone or with Shift,
# MacCtrl as Ctrl, any number of shortcuts, and a command without a description
# or a key for this platform; the shortcuts page takes them too (function_keys.cc).
# A Manifest V2 event page that asks for webRequest runs as a persistent page.
# A page_action next to a browser_action or action, or in Manifest V3, is a
# second action that pageAction.* reaches (firefox_page_action.h).
# menus.create and update take Firefox's icons (not shown) and its password,
# bookmark and tools_menu contexts (the item shows in its other contexts).
# browserSettings is Firefox's, read-only: each setting is a types.ChromeSetting,
# and the preference functions hand its key to firefox_browser_settings.cc.
# So is privacy.network.peerConnectionEnabled, which is always true.
# storage.session is there in Manifest V2 add-ons too, as in Firefox.
# So is the scripting permission, as in Firefox.
# Firefox's theme API, read-only: getCurrent() and onUpdated give the browser
# theme's colours in Firefox's key names (theme_api.cc), to any add-on.
# browserAction has action's setBadgeTextColor, getBadgeTextColor and
# getUserSettings, as in Firefox (firefox_browser_action.h).
# browserAction's and action's setters and getters take Firefox's windowId: a
# value for the tabs of one window (firefox_action_window.h).
# contentScripts.register() is Firefox's (firefox_content_scripts.cc): dynamic
# content scripts, with code as well as files, that the scripting API doesn't
# list or remove. Chromium's contentScripts schema, which only types the
# content_scripts manifest key, is left out of the schemas the bindings read.
# proxy.onRequest and proxy.onError are Firefox's (proxy_internal_api.h): while an
# add-on listens, ProxyConfigMonitor's settings are a PAC script that
# ChromeMojoProxyResolverFactory answers by asking the add-on
# (firefox_proxy_config.h); proxy_custom_bindings.js reports its answer.
# setIcon rejects on the browser's error for a Firefox add-on, as in Firefox
# (set_icon.js); other extensions keep Chromium's promise, which resolves.
# A same-document navigation to about:blank has its document's origin in its
# UrlInfo, not the initiator's, so an add-on's tabs.update() to about:blank#ref
# on a new tab passes navigation_request.cc's and navigator.cc's site checks.
# privacy.network.networkPredictionEnabled set to true turns prediction on: it
# wrote kDefault, which the upstreams make kDisabled.
